## 1. The evidence

### [CXC-STORE-EVIDENCE-FORK] The path-containment guard is a 3-way fork, already drifted

- observation: the identical containment invariant — a request-supplied path segment resolving strictly inside its storage root, or 403/400 — is implemented THREE separate times: `services/bus_storage/server.py`, `services/document_store/server.py`, and `plugins/document_store.py`. [CXC-DOCSTORE-CONTAINMENT]'s own `not-shared-with-bus_storage` property already records the document_store copy as deliberate, not accidental — a second implementation, hand-kept in sync.
- consequence: consolidation de-forks this permanently — ONE store means ONE containment guard, not three implementations of the same rule that can (and, per [INSIGHT-FORK-DIVERGENCE-IS-A-CLASS], will) drift.
- see-also: [INSIGHT-FORK-DIVERGENCE-IS-A-CLASS]
- see-also: [CXC-BUSSTORE-CONTAINMENT]
- see-also: [CXC-DOCSTORE-CONTAINMENT]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-STORE]

### [CXC-STORE-EVIDENCE-COLOCATION] The two services already share one data root

- observation: on NODERED_HOST, document_store's `docstore.db` plus its `blobs/` directory already live INSIDE bus_storage's `/var/lib/composeio` directory, alongside `assets/`. They are two HTTP faces over one co-located filesystem tree today, not two independently-deployed stores that happen to resemble each other.
- consequence: the data move at cutover is near-nil — see [CXC-STORE-DATA-ABSORPTION] for the caveat that "near-nil" still requires a fresh verified copy, not a trust-the-existing-tree assumption.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-STORE]

### [CXC-STORE-EVIDENCE-SCALE] bus_storage is the live, load-bearing store; document_store is a thin catalog

- bus_storage-scale: 1.5 GB across 89 files — device scene `.compose.yaml` files fetched over `bus://assets/displays/*` and booted by devices, plus fonts, icons and CIC screen art. This is the natural survivor: it is what devices actually depend on to boot.
- document_store-scale: 46 docs across 10 collections, 26 blobs — apps, surfaces, board_profiles, components, bsg_planets, recipes, fleet_devices. A catalog, not a load-bearing fleet dependency.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-STORE]

---

## 2. What `doc://` bundled, and where each part goes

### [CXC-STORE-CAP-GET] get-by-id becomes a bus:// blob at a path — no loss

- disposition: `doc://c/id` becomes `bus://c/id.json` — a JSON blob at a path. No capability lost; this is a pure re-addressing.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-STORE]

### [CXC-STORE-CAP-LIST] list-a-collection becomes a bus:// prefix-list — realized via a new /api/v1/blob route reusing the existing listing helper

- disposition: `doc://` listing a collection becomes a `bus://` prefix-list — `bus://apps/` returning ids. This is realized by a new `/api/v1/blob` route family in `services/bus_storage/server.py`, rooted at `BLOB_ROOT = DATA_DIR`, whose prefix-LIST endpoint is built on the shared `_list_one_level()` helper — the SAME helper that backs asset listing (`ASSETS_DIR`), extracted from the asset-listing handler so the two listing shapes cannot drift.
- citation-correction: CORRECTED (diamond gap-analyzer P2, 2026-09-02). This node previously named `list_namespace_ids()` as the realization of the `bus://` prefix-list. That was FACTUALLY WRONG: `list_namespace_ids()` lists the compiled CONFIG namespace registry (`config_dir/*.bin`), an unrelated surface — not doc blobs. No code changed by this correction; it is a spec-text fix only.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-STORE]

### [CXC-STORE-CAP-QUERY] where-clause query — the one capability bus:// lacks, usage is thin

- what-bus-lacks: server-side `where`-clause filtering (`query_docs(collection, where=...)`). This is the ONE capability `bus://` does not already have a shape for.
- usage-is-thin: ONE real caller — the `DocPicker` widget — over a 46-doc store whose largest collection holds 5 docs.
- see-also: [DD-STORE-QUERY-DISPOSITION]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-STORE]

### [DD-STORE-QUERY-DISPOSITION] Drop the server-side query engine — DECIDED, ratified by David

- decision: DROP the server-side query engine and the second SQLite doc DB. `DocPicker` filters CLIENT-SIDE instead — list + get + filter, trivial at 46 docs / 5-doc largest collection, and more decentralization-aligned: the leaf owns its own filtering rather than a coordinator owning it on the leaf's behalf. [CXC-BUS-QUERY] (the platform's existing bus-native query responder) is the escape hatch IF server-side query is ever needed at scale.
- lifecycle-position: decided
- status: decided
- resolution: ratified by David — DSD D1 = A ("Ratify the query-drop now"), 2026-09-03. See `dsd-source`.
- dsd-source: docs/architecture/DSD-storage-consolidation.md
- rationale: usage of the dropped capability is thin — ONE real caller ([CXC-STORE-CAP-QUERY]'s `DocPicker`) over a 46-doc store whose largest collection holds 5 docs — so a server-side query engine is disproportionate machinery to carry forward into the unified store, and dropping it removes a second SQLite doc DB the consolidation would otherwise have to keep alive for one thin caller.
- honest-residual: server-side field-filtering goes away. This is named plainly rather than absorbed silently — a real capability is being dropped, not merely relocated.
- default-order: client-side `query_docs` (`python/composeio/resolve.py`) has NO default temporal order — callers must pass `order_by` if order matters. The retired server engine defaulted to `ORDER BY updated_at DESC`; `updated_at` was server-row metadata, never preserved in the JSON-blob store, so newest-first is not even reconstructable client-side from the blob alone. [CXC-STORE-EV-04] (DocPicker equivalence) HOLDS regardless — `DocPicker` always passes `order_by`; the only caller that omits it, admin's `compact_query`, uses the returned order for a displayed string only, not for selection — benign. Diamond finding: qa note 1, 2026-09-02.
- alternatives: keep the server-side query engine and carry it into the unified store as a genuine second query surface — rejected because usage is thin enough (one caller, 46 docs) that the machinery does not pay for itself; OR build the [CXC-BUS-QUERY] responder now instead of on-demand later — rejected in favor of building it on-demand, see `invalidating-assumption`.
- invalidating_assumption: every collection stays small enough that a leaf-side filter in `DocPicker` is cheap. If any collection grows large enough that client-side filtering hurts, the answer is the platform's EXISTING bus-native query mechanism, [CXC-BUS-QUERY] (a query responder OVER the store) — NOT a second storage interface. That growth is the condition that reopens this decision.
- flagged-for-ratify: no — ratified 2026-09-03. See `resolution` and `dsd-source`.
- see-also: [CXC-STORE-CAP-QUERY]
- see-also: [CXC-BUS-QUERY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-STORE]

